    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d556">

                <pb facs="0618" n="520" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg520"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 Vikner, Nyhus, et al/18: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow (Adams) to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-07-02T20:00:00+08:00">July 2, 1932—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 2—9:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>76. My 75, July 2, 3 p.m.<anchor n="66" xml:id="d556-f1"/> Vikner and Nyhus were
                    released on July 1st at Hsiatien, Hupeh, following conference of communist <pb
                        facs="0619" n="521" xml:id="pg521"/> leaders who stated that they gave the
                    captives freedom because the latter belonged to poor missions and had done
                    nothing inimical to communism. Memorandum of interesting statement of Vikner and
                    Nyhus follows by mail.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d556-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Adams</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
